1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
I've been reading your personal statement, PAUL.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:05,750
First, let's talk about your work experience in South America.

3
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:07,065
What took you there?

4
00:00:07,065 --> 00:00:09,310
Was it to gain more fluency in Spanish?

5
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:14,155
Well, as I'm combining Spanish with Latin American studies, my main idea was to

6
00:00:14,155 --> 00:00:17,270
find out more about the way people lived there.

7
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:20,250
My spoken Spanish was already pretty good in fact.

8
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:22,910
So you weren't too worried about language barriers?

9
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:22,960
No.

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:27,472
In fact, I ended up teaching English there, although that wasn't my original choice

11
00:00:27,472 --> 00:00:28,080
of work.

12
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:28,718
I see.

13
00:00:28,718 --> 00:00:31,270
How did you find out about all this?

14
00:00:31,270 --> 00:00:35,600
I found an agency that runs all kinds of voluntary projects in South America.

15
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,000
What kind of work?

16
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,350
Well, there were several possibilities.

17
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:40,646
You mean construction?

18
00:00:40,646 --> 00:00:41,510
Engineering work?

19
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:44,940
Yes, getting involved in building projects was an option.

20
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:49,150
Then there was tourism - taking tourists for walks around the volcanoes - which

21
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:50,904
I actually chose to do,

22
00:00:50,904 --> 00:00:53,710
and then there was work with local farmers.

23
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:56,642
But you didn't continue with that project.

24
00:00:56,642 --> 00:00:57,480
Why not?

25
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:00,850
Because I never really knew whether I'd be needed or not.

26
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:02,929
I'd thought it might be difficult physically,

27
00:01:02,929 --> 00:01:07,384
but I was certainly fit enough.., no,I wanted to do something that had more

28
00:01:07,384 --> 00:01:09,760
of a proper structure to it, I suppose.

29
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,680
I get de-motivated otherwise.

30
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:15,000
What do you think you learned from your experience?

31
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,940
It must have been a great opportunity to examine community life.

32
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:18,238
Yes,

33
00:01:18,238 --> 00:01:21,820
but it was difficult at first to be accepted by the locals.

34
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:25,418
It was a very remote village and some of them were reluctant to speak

35
00:01:25,418 --> 00:01:27,987
to me - although they were always interested in my clothes

36
00:01:27,987 --> 00:01:30,300
and how much I'd had to pay for them.

37
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:30,767
Well,

38
00:01:30,767 --> 00:01:31,700
that's understandable.

39
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:32,136
Yes,

40
00:01:32,136 --> 00:01:33,880
but things soon improved.

41
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:37,490
What struck me was that when people became more comfortable with me

42
00:01:37,490 --> 00:01:41,400
and less suspicious, we really connected with each other in a meaningful way.

43
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:42,910
You made good friends?

44
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:45,480
Yes, with two of the families in particular.

45
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:45,872
Good.

46
00:01:45,872 --> 00:01:47,048
What about management.

47
00:01:47,048 --> 00:01:49,400
Did you have a project manager?

48
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,760
Yes and he gave me lots of advice and guidance.

49
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:54,940
And was he good at managing too?

50
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:56,840
That wasn't his strong point!

51
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:02,160
I think he was often more interested in the academic side of things than

52
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:02,920
filing reports.

53
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,670
He was a bit of a dreamer.

54
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:07,450
And did you have a contract?

55
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:10,300
I had to stay for a minimum of three months.

56
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,888
My parents were surprised when I asked to stay longer - six months in

57
00:02:13,888 --> 00:02:14,440
the end.

58
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:15,820
I was so happy there.

59
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:19,364
And did anything on the administration side of things surprise you?

60
00:02:19,364 --> 00:02:21,620
What was the food and lodging like?

61
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:21,900
Simple...

62
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:25,827
but there was plenty to eat and I only paid seven dollars a day

63
00:02:25,827 --> 00:02:27,510
for that which was amazing really.

64
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:30,670
And they gave me all the equipment I needed.., even a laptop.

65
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:32,400
You didn't expect that then?

66
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:32,970
No.

67
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:35,520
Well, I'll look forward to hearing more.

68
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:37,650
But now let's look at these modules.

69
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:41,910
You'll need to strat thinking about which ones you will definitely want to study.

70
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:45,900
The first one here is Gender Studies in Latin America.

71
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:46,650
Mmm...

72
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:51,450
It looks at how gender analysis is reconfiguring civil society in Latin America.

73
00:02:51,450 --> 00:02:56,834
Women are increasingly occupying positions in government and in other elected leadership positions in

74
00:02:56,834 --> 00:02:57,603
Latin America.

75
00:02:57,603 --> 00:02:59,910
I think you'd find it interesting.

76
00:02:59,910 --> 00:03:02,894
If it was to do with people in the villages rather than those in

77
00:03:02,894 --> 00:03:03,960
the public sphere, I would.

78
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:04,667
Okay.

79
00:03:04,667 --> 00:03:08,200
What about Second Language Acquisition?

80
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,060
Do you think I'd find that useful?

81
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:14,510
Well, you've had some practical experience in the field, I think it would be.

82
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:15,883
I hadn't thought about that.

83
00:03:15,883 --> 00:03:18,080
I'll put that down as a definite, then.

84
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:21,227
Okay, what about Indigenous Women's lives.

85
00:03:21,227 --> 00:03:22,800
That sounds appropriate.

86
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:23,929
I thought so too,

87
00:03:23,929 --> 00:03:27,600
but I looked at last year's exam questions and that changed my mind.

88
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,544
Don't judge the value of the course on that.

89
00:03:30,544 --> 00:03:35,123
Maybe, talk to some other students first and we can talk about it again

90
00:03:35,123 --> 00:03:35,450
later.

91
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:36,250
Okay.

92
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:36,691
Yes.

93
00:03:36,691 --> 00:03:40,660
And lastly, will you sign up for Portuguese lessons?

94
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:41,936
My Spanish is good,

95
00:03:41,936 --> 00:03:44,170
so would I find that module easy?

96
00:03:44,170 --> 00:03:44,964
Not necessarily.

97
00:03:44,964 --> 00:03:51,318
Some people find that Spanish interferes with learning Portuguese...getting the accent right too.It's quite

98
00:03:51,318 --> 00:03:53,700
different in a lot of ways.

99
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:56,330
Well,I'd much sooner do something else, then.

100
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:56,771
Alright.

101
00:03:56,771 --> 00:03:59,860
Now, what we need to do is...
